Nicola Sturgeon warning that dropping  this stay-at-home message could be a  catastrophe if done too early do you  broadly agree with that but this is my  point it's addressed to both Boris  Johnson and it's addressed to Nicola  Sturgeon whatever you do in the future  is dependent on whether you have got  some protection for people and that  means you've got a test and my complaint  is in Scotland as in the UK only one and  a half percent of the population have  been tested for this virus that it's  difficult to end the lockdown but it's  also difficult to give people  reassurance and it's going to be  difficult to get the rate of infection  down unless you could assure yourself  that people have been tested and are not  carrying the virus and both the Scottish  government and the UK government are  failing to do this and it's become so  obvious to me because I I haven't wanted  to speak out in criticism I've wanted  always to be constructive but if you  have all people's forms as we have in  Scotland and half the deaths are coming  out of old people's homes and in half  the old people's homes and more there  are people with this disease if you  don't test people until after you've got  a case then you are simply trying to  solve a problem after it has been  created you've got to have proactive  testing it's got to be preventative  testing it's got to be regular and  routine and to be fair to every care  worker and to be every resident to be  fair to them and also to be fair to  people in the health service we should  have routine testing and before Boris  Johnson makes more announcements and  before Nicola Sturgeon does anything  else she should address and he should  address the failure to test enough  people so that we cannot be sure who has  the disease and we cannot be sure  because symptoms are being carried that  are not obvious to people and people are  infecting people without us knowing it  right so just to be clear you're saying  not enough testing yet for Boris Johnson  to ease the lockdown at all either even  slightly I think both of them have got  to address this problem that you cannot  reassure the public you're asking people  at some point to go out and make contact  with other people but you're also trying  to get the rate of infection down if  you're not testing  efficiently and remember the chief  medical officer and the chief scientific  officer admitted that not enough testing  had been done at beginning as I say I  haven't wanted to come in on this but  when I see that only one and a half  percent of the British population are  being tested then even at the best Boris  Johnson's two hundred thousand figure a  day would be testing only not 0.3  percent of the population in Scotland  the figure is actually less ambitious  than Boris Johnson's new figure it's  just not good enough the only way when  you don't have a vaccination if there  was a vaccination you'd tell everybody  to get vaccinated in the absence of a  vaccine you've got to test and you've  got to find out who is carrying this  disease and how you can isolate them  quickly from the rest of the public but  of course if people are going to if  people are staying at home and don't  have the disease then you can actually  test them and they could get back to  work right but I mean if you believe the  papers here like headlines like happy  Monday and you know that a sense that  there is gonna be a slight easing even  from Monday all the of the lock down do  you think that you think that could be a  reckless way to start things did but  mark you're getting obsessed about a  story that is not even been announced  yet it's all speculation I don't think  Boris Johnson can make an announcement  on Sunday in the way that these papers  are saying so without ramping up testing  massively so let's concentrate on the  problem rather than on headlines that  actually mean very little until we see  the results and everybody's reacting to  stories that are speculation what you've  got to do is deal with the real problem  people are dying in all people's homes  and people are dying elsewhere and one  of the reasons they're dying is the  failure to test it's both wrong and it's  tragic and I feel sorry for health  workers who are being dragged into  situations where they are trying to do  their best to save lives and putting  their lives at risk  because we haven't tested them I talked  to one of the heads of an old people's  home in Scotland today who runs actually  15 homes and he told me that he had  1,100 staff and he'd only been able to  get 162 tested he had 700 there patients  and only 192 had being tested and this  in a situation where everybody is saying  that  at the epicenter of the crisis he told  me about people who were carrying  symptoms and he had not been able to get  them tested that this is something that  could be dealt with today and tomorrow  and the report that we put out today  from our Scottish future says what can  be done what you do is you ramp up your  manufacturing capacity just as if you  were it was a wartime effort you ramp up  your manufacturing country we figure  companies that are necessary to produce  the testing equipment then what you do  is of course you have centers all over  the country far more than we've got at  the moment mobile testing home testing  workplace testing you get it done and  then the next thing to do is you get the  laboratory capacity properly used and of  course there are spaces that are not  being used at the moment that could  actually be brought into use now if you  had a wartime mobilization strategy that  was dealing with this then you could  ramp up the number of tests by hundreds  of thousands every day and that in my  view and the absence of a vaccination is  going to become essential and anybody  who wants to talk about the next few  days has got to talk and explain why if  they're going to make changes or if  they're not going to make changes in the  niccola sturgeons case they have failed  on testing and they continue to fail on  that front okay you're a former  Chancellor you know better than I do  that business is gonna be crying out to  get back to work particularly the Bank  of England's warning about the sharpest  recession on record there is this  argument that we just simply need to get  businesses back operating look the  economy exists for people people don't  exist for the economy we know that you  cannot solve this economic emergency  unless you start to solve the medical  emergency we know that unless you can  actually get people properly protected  against this virus you cannot get  everybody in the way that you want back  to work so let's deal with the health  emergency let's get it sorted why is it  after three months that ninety eight and  a half percent of the population have  not being tested if this was a  vaccination you would be demanding a  hundred percent vaccination coverage by  now why when in the absence of a  vaccination we're relying on testing to  know how we can avoid people being in  contact with the disease we don't  the testing and the contract tracing  then the isolation of course we need the  hand-washing and of course we also need  the social distancing but there's a  missing element in this that you can't  sort the economic problem unless you've  got some progress on the health problem  that is good enough to be able to give  people confidence that they can come  into contact with other people but isn't  the point here that the casualties of a  prolonged shutdown could be every bit as  as bad as the casualties from from the  virus in the in the circumstances were  in well are you wanting to put lives  second to livelihoods you cannot do that  to mark you've got to deal with the  health emergency and really I've had to  enter this debate because people are  talking in abstract terms about the next  year a few a few weeks and there's all  these headlines today but the reality is  when you look at the figures about  what's actually happening on the ground  we are leaving people far too vulnerable  and we've got to do something about it  now I've got plenty ideas about how we  can bring the economy back and I know  from my own experience in 2008/9 you  will have to do it without causing the  levels of unemployment that people are  predicting the levels of bankruptcies  they're inevitable if we do what he's  being proposed at the moment the levels  of mortgage repossessions that we saw in  the 80s and the 90s but we avoided in  2009-10 now there's plenty of ways that  we can plan to do this and do it  purposefully but you cannot do it  successfully  unless you've dealt with the medical  problem but are you do you agree that  the way Russia sunic is handling with it  handling this is broadly right that they  are throwing money at it well I was the  one who said look in his first set of  proposals he had missed out employment  and then of course he did the right  thing and he guaranteed that people's  employment would be safeguarded  but that's only stage one of the crisis  look stage two is dealing with what  happens when the employment subsidy is  starting to either run out or it's  removed altogether and when the banks  are calling in the loans that have been  given because it's only loans in the  main there are some grants but mainly  loans that have been given to companies  now we've got to deal with a situation  where unless there is a second phase of  gotten an action unless they are cordon  the way they approach the recovery then  large numbers of businesses that may be  viable over the next year but can't  create sufficient revenues over the next  three months will be brought down either  by the banks or by the Inland Revenue  who are the fat authorities unless we  were able to do something to help them  so yes we need an economic plan the easy  part in a sense economically has been  done you hand out money you you say to  people we can reassure you for the next  three months but the difficult part as  the Bank of England would I think  emphasize is you've got to have a plan  to keep unemployment low and you've got  have a plan to stop business  bankruptcies and I think I know that  there are ways that we can do this but  first of all you have got to sort out  the medical emergency yeah just a couple  more I mean broadly speaking obviously  it's a crisis of huge challenges for any  government what do you make broadly of  the way this is being handled given the  trajectory that we're on well I want the  government to succeed in this and the  whole country wants the government to  succeed I want the UK government and the  Scottish government to secede that's why  I want to be constructive and not  critical and I obviously want to look  forward with proposals that I'm making  today and not look back but unless the  government listens to the proposals that  we are making about testing and unless  they match that to the contract tracing  and to the isolation policies and put  that complimentary to social distancing  and the way they move that forward we  will still have both the medical and the  economic problem some months from now I  want to avoid a second wave and a second  peak but the waiter avoided as you are  trying to come out of your first peak is  is in my view and I come back to it  because we have looked at studies that  have been done in America and elsewhere  studies that are being done in Europe  and we have been let's be honest we have  been testing about half the population  as a percentage that the countries that  have been more successful Germany Norway  Denmark Korea and we mean half as  successful as doing that testing as  these other countries and we've got to  learn some of the lessons from what has  been more successful than what has  happened in this country so I don't want  to be critical but I want to put forward  constructive proposals of  what can be done and I don't think  anybody should be in any doubt today  that you cannot avoid a second peak  unless you do more about testing okay  and just finally you made great play  early on in this crisis of global  cooperation isn't the truth that it's  basically every country for itself and  there's precious little global  cooperation well if we're going to get  out of this and eradicate this disease  an entirety we're going to need global  cooperation because unless the vaccine  is in every country it's not going to  make us altogether safe and unless we  build up capacity to deal with these  problems in the poorest countries  they're going to come back to haunt us  in the richest countries I've been  disappointed by the level of global  cooperation there was a pledging  conference but I was pleased that Boris  Johnson was part of that on Monday to  raise money for the vaccine and to build  up the capacity in our health care  system and globally but certainly more  has got to be done and we must not  retreat into this America first China  first India first Russia first the sort  of approaches that have been taken for  too long the evidence is that if one  area merits multilateral cooperation and  has got to can only work with  multilateral cooperation it's dealing  with a pandemic

